__registerContent("gs2/polity/laxmikanth/part1/ch3/classification", {"notes":{"ch3-classification":{"era":"Laxmikanth Part I, Chapter 3","significance":["Classification types and their country-examples are among the most tested Prelims match-the-following topics","The Rigid/Flexible classification (Lord James Bryce) and India's position as 'neither rigid nor flexible but synthesis of both' is directly testable","K.C. Wheare's description of India as 'quasi-federal' is a classic Prelims and Mains answer point","Procedural vs Prescriptive (Elliot Bulmer) is increasingly relevant as UPSC shifts toward conceptual questions"],"features":[{"key":"Classification 1: Evolved vs Enacted","main":"EVOLVED: outcome of slow and gradual evolutionary process; provisions in form of conventions, practices, principles, judicial decisions; also called 'cumulative constitution'; Example: British Constitution\nENACTED: deliberately made by a constituent assembly/constitutional council or promulgated by a king or parliament; provisions in form of a book/document; also called 'conventional constitution'; Examples: American and Indian Constitutions","note":""},{"key":"Classification 2: Written vs Unwritten","main":"WRITTEN: provisions incorporated in form of a book/document/series of documents; consciously formulated and adopted; also called 'documentary' or 'codified' constitution; Examples: USA, Canada, Japan, France, India\nUNWRITTEN: most provisions NOT in a document; found in conventions, practices, charters, statutes, judicial decisions; product of historical evolution; also called 'non-documentary' or 'uncodified' constitution; Examples: UK, New Zealand, Israel","note":"The distinction between Written/Unwritten is more or less the SAME as Enacted/Evolved"},{"key":"Classification 3: Rigid vs Flexible","main":"Classification given by LORD JAMES BRYCE\nRIGID: cannot be amended in the same manner as ordinary laws; requires SPECIAL PROCEDURE for amendment; distinguishes between constitutional law and ordinary law; also called 'inelastic constitution'; Examples: USA, Australia, Switzerland\nFLEXIBLE: can be amended in the same manner as ordinary laws; NO special procedure required; does NOT distinguish between constitutional law and ordinary law; also called 'elastic constitution'; Examples: UK, New Zealand\nINDIA: neither rigid nor flexible — a SYNTHESIS OF BOTH","note":""},{"key":"Classification 4: Federal vs Unitary","main":"FEDERAL: provides for DIVISION OF POWER between national government and regional governments; each operates independently in their respective jurisdictions; Examples: USA, Switzerland, Australia, Canada, Russia, Brazil\nUNITARY: provides for CONCENTRATION OF POWER in the national government; regional governments are SUBORDINATE AGENCIES; Exercise only delegated powers; Examples: UK, France, Japan, China, Italy, Norway\nINDIA: contains both federal AND unitary provisions; described as 'QUASI-FEDERAL' by K.C. Wheare","note":""},{"key":"Classification 5: Procedural vs Prescriptive","main":"Classification by ELLIOT BULMER (constitutional scholar)\nPROCEDURAL: defines legal and political structures of public institutions; sets out legal limits of government power to protect democratic processes and fundamental human rights\nPRESCRIPTIVE: in addition to describing how government functions, it assumes (or attempts to impose) a broad consensus on common societal goals that public authorities must strive to achieve","note":"India's Constitution has BOTH — procedural (defining institutions, rights, separation of powers) AND prescriptive (DPSPs committing to social-economic goals)"},{"key":"India's Position in Each Classification","main":"Evolved vs Enacted: ENACTED (deliberately made by Constituent Assembly)\nWritten vs Unwritten: WRITTEN/CODIFIED (world's longest written constitution)\nRigid vs Flexible: SYNTHESIS OF BOTH (Article 368: three-tier amendment process)\nFederal vs Unitary: QUASI-FEDERAL (K.C. Wheare); federal with unitary features\nProcedural vs Prescriptive: BOTH (procedural institutions + prescriptive DPSPs)","note":""}],"traps":[{"label":"Evolved = Cumulative; Enacted = Conventional","lines":["Evolved constitution = also called CUMULATIVE constitution","Enacted constitution = also called CONVENTIONAL constitution","Common trap: reversing these — thinking 'conventional' means 'traditional' (evolved), when it actually means 'by convention/agreement' (enacted)"]},{"label":"Written = Documentary/Codified; Unwritten = Non-documentary/Uncodified","lines":["Written = 'documentary' OR 'codified' constitution","Unwritten = 'non-documentary' OR 'uncodified' constitution","Israel and New Zealand = UNWRITTEN/UNCODIFIED (no single constitutional document)","Common trap: assuming UK is the only unwritten constitution — New Zealand and Israel are also examples"]},{"label":"Lord James Bryce: Rigid vs Flexible","lines":["The Rigid/Flexible classification was given by LORD JAMES BRYCE","Rigid = also called INELASTIC; examples: USA, Australia, Switzerland","Flexible = also called ELASTIC; examples: UK, New Zealand","India = synthesis of both (NOT purely rigid, NOT purely flexible)"]},{"label":"K.C. Wheare: Quasi-Federal","lines":["K.C. Wheare described India as QUASI-FEDERAL","Wheare was a British constitutional scholar; wrote 'Federal Government' (1946)","Wheare's test: true federation needs coordinate (equal) Centre and States; India's Centre is stronger → quasi-federal","Do NOT confuse: Wheare gave the 'quasi-federal' label for India BUT gave the 'rigid/flexible/written/unwritten' framework separately"]},{"label":"Elliot Bulmer: Two Roles","lines":["Bulmer identified BOTH the 8 Functions (Chapter 3) AND the Procedural/Prescriptive classification","Common trap: attributing either Bryce's or Bulmer's work to the other","Bryce: Rigid/Flexible (19th century scholar); Bulmer: Procedural/Prescriptive + 8 Functions (contemporary scholar)"]},{"label":"Country Examples — Frequently Tested","lines":["Federal: USA, Switzerland, Australia, Canada, Russia, Brazil","Unitary: UK, France, Japan, China, Italy, Norway","Rigid/Inelastic: USA, Australia, Switzerland","Flexible/Elastic: UK, New Zealand","Written/Codified: USA, Canada, Japan, France, India","Unwritten/Uncodified: UK, New Zealand, Israel"]}],"mainsAngle":"India's constitutional classification across all five dimensions reveals a document of remarkable synthesis: enacted yet reflecting evolutionary wisdom (incorporating best practices from 60 constitutions); written yet with unwritten conventions (cabinet conventions, money bill practices); rigid for essential provisions yet flexible for routine amendments (three-tier Article 368); federal in structure yet unitary in spirit (quasi-federal, K.C. Wheare); and both procedural (defining institutions, rights) and prescriptive (DPSPs committing to social goals). This 'neither this nor that' character across multiple dimensions is not a weakness but a deliberate design choice — reflecting the founders' rejection of rigid Western categories in favour of an Indian synthesis suited to the country's unique historical, social, and economic context.","mainsTheme":"Constitutional classification — India's unique position as a synthesis across all five dimensions","recall":["Evolved = cumulative; Enacted = conventional; British = evolved; Indian/American = enacted","Written = documentary/codified (USA, Canada, Japan, France, India); Unwritten = non-documentary/uncodified (UK, New Zealand, Israel)","Rigid/Flexible classification: LORD JAMES BRYCE","Rigid = inelastic (USA, Australia, Switzerland); Flexible = elastic (UK, New Zealand)","India: neither rigid nor flexible — SYNTHESIS OF BOTH","Federal vs Unitary: based on Centre-State power relationship","Federal: USA, Switzerland, Australia, Canada, Russia, Brazil","Unitary: UK, France, Japan, China, Italy, Norway","India = QUASI-FEDERAL by K.C. Wheare","Procedural vs Prescriptive: ELLIOT BULMER","India: both procedural AND prescriptive"]}},"quizzes":{"ch3-classification":{"title":"Classification of Constitutions","questions":[{"q":"The classification of constitutions into 'Rigid' and 'Flexible' was given by:","options":["K.C. Wheare","Lord James Bryce","Elliot Bulmer","A.V. Dicey"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Rigid/Flexible classification was given by LORD JAMES BRYCE — a British jurist, historian, and statesman (1838-1922), famous for his work 'The American Commonwealth' (1888)."},{"q":["An evolved constitution is also known as a:","A flexible constitution is also known as a:","Match the correct pair:"],"options":["Evolved = Conventional; Flexible = Inelastic","Evolved = Cumulative; Flexible = Elastic","Evolved = Codified; Flexible = Cumulative","Evolved = Documentary; Flexible = Inelastic"],"correct":1,"explain":"EVOLVED = CUMULATIVE (accumulates gradually through conventions and practices). FLEXIBLE = ELASTIC (can be stretched/amended like ordinary law). These are the chapter's explicit alternative names."},{"q":["Consider the following pairs (Constitution type — Alternative name):","I. Evolved constitution — Cumulative constitution","II. Enacted constitution — Conventional constitution","III. Written constitution — Codified or Documentary constitution","IV. Flexible constitution — Inelastic constitution","How many of the above pairs are correctly matched?"],"options":["Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs","Only one pair"],"correct":1,"explain":"Pairs I, II, and III are correctly matched. Pair IV is INCORRECT — Flexible = ELASTIC (not inelastic). Rigid = Inelastic. Flexible = Elastic. This is a classic reversal trap."},{"q":"Which of the following countries has an UNWRITTEN (non-documentary/uncodified) constitution?","options":["France","Japan","Canada","New Zealand"],"correct":3,"explain":"New Zealand has an unwritten/uncodified constitution — it has no single constitutional document but operates through a collection of statutes, conventions, and common law. France, Japan, and Canada all have written/codified constitutions."},{"q":["Israel has an unwritten constitution. This means:","I. Israel has absolutely no laws governing its government's structure.","II. Israel's constitution is found in its Basic Laws, conventions, and judicial decisions rather than a single document.","III. Israel has been trying to draft a written constitution since its founding in 1948 but has not yet succeeded.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["II only","I and III only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":2,"explain":"Statements II and III are correct. Statement I is incorrect — having an unwritten constitution does NOT mean having no laws; it means the constitutional framework is found in multiple sources rather than one document. Israel has ongoing constitutional debates since 1948."},{"q":"A RIGID constitution requires a special procedure for amendment because:","options":["Its provisions are too complex to be amended by ordinary procedures","It distinguishes between constitutional law (higher) and ordinary law — constitutional changes need higher levels of consent","The framers were too conservative to allow any change","International law prohibits easy amendment of constitutions"],"correct":1,"explain":"A rigid constitution distinguishes between constitutional law (higher, supreme) and ordinary law — this distinction requires that changes to the higher law (constitution) need a more demanding procedure than changes to ordinary law, reflecting the constitution's supreme status."},{"q":"The Constitution of India is described as 'neither rigid nor flexible but a synthesis of both.' Which provision BEST demonstrates this synthesis?","options":["The Preamble's commitment to Justice, Liberty, Equality and Fraternity","Article 368 which provides three different levels of amendment procedure depending on the provision being amended","The Basic Structure Doctrine which makes the constitution permanently rigid","Article 13 which makes all inconsistent laws void"],"correct":1,"explain":"Article 368's three-tier amendment procedure is the synthesis: (1) some provisions by simple majority (flexible), (2) most provisions by special majority (moderately rigid), (3) certain provisions by special majority + state ratification (rigid). This graduated approach is the 'synthesis' the chapter refers to."},{"q":"K.C. Wheare described India's Constitution as 'quasi-federal' rather than 'federal' because:","options":["India has both written and unwritten constitutional elements","India's Centre is stronger than the States, departing from Wheare's test of a true federation requiring coordinate (equal) authority between Centre and States","India has both procedural and prescriptive constitutional provisions","India's Constitution can be amended by both simple and special majority"],"correct":1,"explain":"Wheare's test for a true federation requires COORDINATE (equal) authority between Centre and States. India's Constitution gives the Centre stronger powers (emergency provisions, Article 356, residuary powers, etc.), making it 'quasi-federal' rather than truly federal per Wheare."},{"q":["Consider the following countries and their constitutional classification:","I. UK — Unwritten, Flexible, Unitary","II. USA — Written, Rigid, Federal","III. Australia — Written, Rigid, Federal","IV. Switzerland — Written, Flexible, Federal","How many of the above are correctly classified?"],"options":["Only two","Only three","All four","Only one"],"correct":1,"explain":"I, II, and III are correctly classified. IV is incorrect — Switzerland has a RIGID constitution (not flexible). The chapter specifically lists Switzerland as an example of a RIGID constitution along with USA and Australia."},{"q":"The chapter states that the distinction between a 'written constitution' and an 'unwritten constitution' is 'more or less the same' as the distinction between which other pair?","options":["Rigid and Flexible constitutions","Federal and Unitary constitutions","Evolved and Enacted constitutions","Procedural and Prescriptive constitutions"],"correct":2,"explain":"The chapter explicitly states: 'The distinction between a written constitution and an unwritten constitution is more or less the same as is between an evolved constitution and an enacted constitution.' Written ≈ Enacted; Unwritten ≈ Evolved."},{"q":"A FEDERAL constitution is fundamentally distinguished from a UNITARY constitution by:","options":["The total length of the constitutional document","Whether the constitution is written or unwritten","The division of powers between national and regional governments (who can operate independently in their respective jurisdictions)","Whether the constitution can be amended by a simple majority"],"correct":2,"explain":"The defining feature of a federal constitution is DIVISION OF POWERS between national and regional governments, with each operating independently in their jurisdictions. A unitary constitution concentrates power in the national government, making regional governments subordinate agencies."},{"q":["In a UNITARY constitution, regional governments:","I. Operate independently in their own jurisdictions","II. Are subordinate agencies of the national government","III. Exercise only such powers as may be delegated to them by the national government","IV. Have the same constitutional status as the national government","Select the correct answer:"],"options":["II and III only","I and IV only","I, II and III only","I, II, III and IV"],"correct":0,"explain":"Statements II and III correctly describe regional governments under a unitary constitution. Statements I and IV describe FEDERAL systems where regional governments operate independently with equal constitutional status."},{"q":"Elliot Bulmer's distinction between PROCEDURAL and PRESCRIPTIVE constitutions relates to:","options":["Whether the constitution uses procedural law or substantive law","The nature and purposes of the constitutional document — whether it only sets up structures and limits (procedural) or also commits to societal goals (prescriptive)","Whether the constitution requires special amendment procedures (procedural) or not (prescriptive)","Whether the constitution was enacted by a procedure (procedural) or evolved organically (prescriptive)"],"correct":1,"explain":"Bulmer's distinction: a PROCEDURAL constitution defines government structures and limits government power to protect democratic processes. A PRESCRIPTIVE constitution goes further — it assumes or imposes a consensus on common societal goals that government must achieve. The difference is whether the constitution goes beyond structure to mandate outcomes."},{"q":["India's Constitution is BOTH procedural AND prescriptive because:","I. It is procedural in defining governmental institutions and structures (Parliament, Supreme Court, President)","II. It is procedural in protecting fundamental human rights (Part III)","III. It is prescriptive in committing the state to social-economic goals through DPSPs (Part IV)","IV. It is prescriptive in declaring India's borders","Select the correct answer:"],"options":["I, II and III only","I and III only","I, II, III and IV","III and IV only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Statements I, II, and III are correct. Statement IV is incorrect — declaring borders is a PROCEDURAL (boundary-defining) function, not prescriptive. India's prescriptive character is embodied in DPSPs committing to social-economic goals."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about constitutional classification:","I. All written constitutions are also enacted constitutions.","II. All evolved constitutions are also unwritten constitutions.","III. A constitution can be both rigid and flexible depending on which provision is being amended.","IV. India is an example of a constitution that is rigid for some provisions and flexible for others.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I and II only","III and IV only","I, II and III only","I, II, III and IV"],"correct":3,"explain":"All four are correct: (I) Written = enacted (consciously formulated); (II) Evolved = unwritten (product of historical evolution); (III) India is the prime example of a constitution that is rigid (special majority + state ratification for some) and more flexible (simple majority for others); (IV) India's Article 368 explicitly demonstrates this."},{"q":["Assertion (A): India's Constitution is described as 'quasi-federal' rather than purely 'federal' by K.C. Wheare.","Reason (R): While the Indian Constitution distributes legislative powers between Centre and States (Seventh Schedule), it also gives the Centre overriding powers during emergencies (Articles 352–360), power to assume state subjects (Article 249), and makes the States financially dependent — making the Centre stronger.","Which one of the following is correct?"],"options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true and R correctly explains A — the specific mechanisms (Emergency provisions, Article 249, financial dependence) are precisely why Wheare found India departing from a true federal model where Centre and States have coordinate authority."},{"q":"Which of the following sets correctly lists countries with a FEDERAL constitution?","options":["UK, France, Japan, China","USA, Switzerland, Australia, Canada, Russia, Brazil","Norway, Italy, France, New Zealand","Japan, South Korea, Singapore, Vietnam"],"correct":1,"explain":"Federal constitutions: USA, Switzerland, Australia, Canada, Russia, Brazil — all mentioned in the chapter. Unitary constitutions: UK, France, Japan, China, Italy, Norway, etc."},{"q":"The chapter mentions that New Zealand and Israel have unwritten constitutions. What do these two countries have in COMMON that accounts for their unwritten constitutional status?","options":["Both are island nations without stable governments","Both have constitutional frameworks found in collections of statutes, conventions, and judicial decisions rather than a single codified document","Both inherited their constitutions from the British colonial system","Both are very small countries that do not need formal constitutional documents"],"correct":1,"explain":"Both New Zealand and Israel have unwritten/uncodified constitutions — their constitutional frameworks exist in collections of laws, conventions, and judicial decisions rather than a single supreme document. While New Zealand inherited British constitutional traditions, Israel chose not to draft a full constitution at independence (1948) due to political difficulties."},{"q":"An 'evolved' constitution is best described as one that:","options":["Is deliberately made by a constituent assembly at a single point in time","Is the outcome of a slow and gradual evolutionary process, expressed through conventions, practices and judicial decisions","Is promulgated by a king or parliament in the form of a single book","Divides power between a national government and regional governments"],"correct":1,"explain":"An evolved constitution (e.g. the British Constitution) is the outcome of a slow, gradual evolutionary process, with its provisions found in conventions, practices, principles and judicial decisions rather than a single deliberately drafted document."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about India's constitutional classification:","I. It is an enacted constitution.","II. It is a written/codified constitution.","III. It is described as quasi-federal.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":3,"explain":"All three are correct — India's Constitution is enacted (made by the Constituent Assembly), written/codified (world's longest written constitution), and described as quasi-federal by K.C. Wheare."},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of an ENACTED constitution?","options":["The British Constitution","The Indian Constitution","The New Zealand Constitution","The Israeli Constitution"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Indian (and American) Constitution is enacted — deliberately made by a constituent body. The British, New Zealand and Israeli constitutions are evolved/unwritten in character."},{"q":["Consider the following pairs (Country — Constitutional feature):","I. Canada — Federal","II. France — Unitary","III. Brazil — Unitary","How many of the above pairs are correctly matched?"],"options":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","All three pairs","None of the pairs"],"correct":1,"explain":"Pairs I and II are correctly matched — Canada is federal, France is unitary. Pair III is incorrect — Brazil is FEDERAL, not unitary."},{"q":"A RIGID constitution is also known as:","options":["An elastic constitution","An inelastic constitution","A cumulative constitution","A conventional constitution"],"correct":1,"explain":"A rigid constitution is also called an 'inelastic' constitution — it cannot be stretched or amended like ordinary law and requires a special amendment procedure. A flexible constitution is 'elastic'."},{"q":["Statement I: A federal constitution provides for a division of powers between the national and regional governments.","Statement II: In a unitary constitution, regional governments exercise only such powers as are delegated to them by the national government.","Which one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?"],"options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both statements are correct — federalism divides power between national and regional governments (each independent in its sphere), while in a unitary system regional governments are subordinate agencies exercising only delegated powers."},{"q":"The 'Procedural vs Prescriptive' classification of constitutions was given by:","options":["Lord James Bryce","K.C. Wheare","Elliot Bulmer","A.V. Dicey"],"correct":2,"explain":"Elliot Bulmer gave the Procedural vs Prescriptive classification. A procedural constitution defines structures and limits on power; a prescriptive one additionally commits the state to common societal goals."},{"q":"Which classification of constitutions did K.C. Wheare use when he described India as 'quasi-federal'?","options":["Written vs Unwritten","Rigid vs Flexible","Federal vs Unitary","Procedural vs Prescriptive"],"correct":2,"explain":"Wheare's 'quasi-federal' description of India belongs to the Federal vs Unitary classification — he found India's Centre stronger than the States, departing from a true (coordinate) federation."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about a written constitution:","I. Its provisions are incorporated in a book, document or a series of documents.","II. It is also called a documentary or codified constitution.","III. It is necessarily flexible, being amendable like ordinary law.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":0,"explain":"Statements I and II are correct — a written constitution is documented/codified. Statement III is incorrect — being written does not make a constitution flexible; the USA and India have written constitutions that are rigid or a synthesis of rigid and flexible."},{"q":"An enacted constitution is also referred to as a:","options":["Cumulative constitution","Conventional constitution","Elastic constitution","Uncodified constitution"],"correct":1,"explain":"An enacted constitution is also called a 'conventional' constitution (deliberately made by agreement/convention). An evolved constitution is the 'cumulative' one — a common reversal trap."}]}}});
